hi there steve kaufman here again to  talk about language learning as usual  and today i want to talk about what  makes a language easy to learn remember  um if you like my videos please  subscribe if you like this video please  give it a like  i'm often asked you know how long does  it take to learn a language which  languages is easiest to learn are some  languages easier than others and so  forth so here's my take on what makes  languages easier  the first thing is how much similar  vocabulary is there okay if you are an  english speaker learning french 50 of  the vocabulary or more is recognizable  so therefore easy to learn  and and i'll explain why that gives you  big advantages um  if you are uh you know even um  i don't know even if you're learning  german there's gonna be i don't know  what the percentage is but there's a lot  of words there that you recognize  and where that's not the case for  example right now i'm struggling with  arabic persian and  turkish they're essentially there are  very few it's a ten percent  uh words that are recognizable from a  european language uh there's another  number of words that i recognize  because they connect arabic and persian  and turkish so they're common to those  three languages and so to that extent  that helps so to me the overwhelming  consideration is the amount of  recognizable vocabulary when i after  learning russian czech has about 60  percent of  vocabulary that is recognizable from  russian  uh that gives you a big head start  um and and the reason that's important  is because the second thing that  influences how difficult or easy a  language is to learn is the available  the availability of interesting  compelling content in that language and  that means that you have to be able to  get from sort of beginner  material material sort of designed for  the learner to material that's genuinely  interesting so i'm trying to and i did  for arabic and for persian try to get to  podcasts and and movies and things that  are genuinely interesting but it's very  difficult because  the gap between the beginner the  beginner materials such as our many  stories where there's a lot of  repetition of vocabulary to get from  that to sort of genuinely interesting  material is a much bigger gap because  there are so few words that you  recognize whereas if you are in french  or spanish or  even having done one slavic language you  go to another slavic language you can  much more quickly  get to a situation as i was in those  languages say in even in polish or in  ukrainian where you can find genuine  genuinely interesting stuff podcasts  whatever that you can listen to take  with you every day and so you're you're  no longer consciously learning the  language you are learning about  interesting things  using the language and your language  skills are developing so to me  that's the big thing how close are the  languages in terms of vocabulary and  therefore how quickly can you get on to  interesting content the other  consideration of course is the  availability of interesting content but  that's increasingly there it's sometimes  difficult to find you can look for it in  the itunes library or on netflix or  you know radio stations online or  anywhere where they have material that  you can  access  uh i for example now i'm looking for  turkish podcasts and i'm gonna put the  podcast or maybe  yeah put the podcast on to uh this happy  scribe or happy transcribe it is this  transcription service  or with our link extension i can take uh  you know youtube videos that have  subtitles  and automatically import them into link  as study material  um you know korean a big obstacle for me  while korean shares a lot of vocabulary  with chinese and japanese to the extent  that they share chinese origin  vocabulary  these are hidden for me in korean  because i don't know what the characters  are  now in link for example in japanese we  have the you can show  on the lesson page you can show the the  furigana in other words the hiragana  sort of phonetic  script for what you are reading or you  can show the romaji so we have the  ability to show another line of text it  would be amazing if in my korean  learning we could get a hold of some  automatic conversion of korean into  kanji into chinese characters where  those characters exist and then to show  that  uh you know on top of the text which is  all in hangul so these would be all  sorts of ways of making genuinely  interesting material more accessible  otherwise it's a big struggle to get  from sort of easy material to genuinely  compelling material especially when  all of the words have to be learned  whereas not all because some of the  words are forms of other words but  you don't have so much common vocabulary  that helps you get sort of to transition  from beginner material to genuinely  compelling material so just my take on  what makes a language difficult it's not  word order it's not even the grammar  to me it's words how  many sort of how many words are  recognizable based on languages that you  know  and that is going to help you transition  to more interesting content which will  then speed you on your way  to learning the language i look forward  to your  comments thank you bye  you
 

